1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:16,070
Imagine a tent at evening, in Beersheba, a long time ago. The old man inside cannot
see. He is waiting for his son.

2
00:00:21,976 --> 00:00:23,370
[Jacob] My father.

3
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:28,010
[Isaac] Here I am. Which one are you, my son?

4
00:00:28,932 --> 00:00:38,823
Which one are you? He knows that voice. He has known it for more than sixty years.
And it is in the wrong place.

5
00:00:38,873 --> 00:00:44,520
Here is Brian, by way of Mister Scaredy Cat, on what a blind man hears in that.

6
00:00:45,070 --> 00:00:49,694
[Brian] A familiar sound where it does not belong is a blind man's alarm.

7
00:00:49,744 --> 00:00:57,340
[Brian] I can be anywhere in my house and know who is moving and roughly what they
are doing, because I know what every sound means and where it comes from.

8
00:00:57,390 --> 00:01:07,330
[Brian] The day something I know turns up where it should not be, I stop. Isaac
stopped. He heard Jacob, in the hour he had set aside for Esau, and he asked.

9
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:12,493
[Brian] That question, which one are you, is the most honest sentence in the
chapter.

10
00:01:12,543 --> 00:01:18,380
[Brian] Everything that follows is the story of how a careful blind man was talked
out of the one sense that was telling him the truth.

11
00:01:22,102 --> 00:01:31,822
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

12
00:01:31,872 --> 00:01:40,249
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

13
00:01:40,299 --> 00:01:42,880
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

14
00:01:43,930 --> 00:01:55,695
This episode is part of our series on sight lost and not given back: six people in
the Bible who lost their sight and lived the rest of their lives without it.

15
00:01:55,745 --> 00:02:12,266
We tell them in three pairs. Two men blinded by their enemies, Zedekiah and Samson.
A father and a son, Isaac and Jacob. Two old men of Shiloh, Eli and Ahijah.

16
00:02:12,316 --> 00:02:18,886
Every episode begins with sounds, and with what a blind man hears in them.

17
00:02:18,936 --> 00:02:30,989
Along the way we follow the Hebrew words the Bible uses for eyes that fail, one word
handing on to the next. When we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

18
00:02:31,039 --> 00:02:38,280
Everything else is from the text, from the old books read in their own voice, and
from Brian.

19
00:02:39,830 --> 00:02:42,414
First, where we are.

20
00:02:42,464 --> 00:02:53,039
Isaac is the son Abraham and Sarah waited twenty-five years for, the boy who carried
the wood up Mount Moriah, asked his father where the lamb was,

21
00:02:53,089 --> 00:02:57,014
and heard him answer that God Himself would provide it.

22
00:02:57,064 --> 00:03:06,286
He was bound on the altar and spared. He learned trust in the Lord from underneath
the knife, and he would need it.

23
00:03:06,336 --> 00:03:17,743
Hebrews puts Abraham on that mountain in its list of the people who lived by faith.
Isaac is in the same list, one verse later, for the chapter we are about to read.

24
00:03:17,793 --> 00:03:28,185
He married Rebekah at forty, and waited twenty more years for the twins. Before they
were born, Rebekah was told that the older would serve the younger.

25
00:03:28,235 --> 00:03:41,592
Esau came out first, red and hairy, and grew into a hunter and his father's
favourite. Jacob came out holding his heel, stayed among the tents, and was his
mother's.

26
00:03:41,642 --> 00:03:49,449
One day Esau came in from the field famished and sold his birthright to Jacob for a
bowl of stew.

27
00:03:49,499 --> 00:04:00,713
By the chapter we are reading, Isaac is past a hundred, Esau is married to two
Hittite women, and Genesis says they brought grief to Isaac and Rebekah.

28
00:04:00,763 --> 00:04:04,458
It does not say how, but it does not need to.

29
00:04:04,508 --> 00:04:15,054
Abraham had made his servant swear that Isaac would not marry a daughter of Canaan,
and Esau, at forty, married two of them and did not care.

30
00:04:15,104 --> 00:04:19,880
And Isaac's eyes have failed. Where this is.

31
00:04:19,930 --> 00:04:29,989
Stand at Beersheba, at the southern edge of the settled land, where Isaac had
reopened his father's wells and named the place, and face north,

32
00:04:30,039 --> 00:04:32,054
with north at twelve o'clock.

33
00:04:32,104 --> 00:04:38,477
This was his country for decades, and Genesis does not say the tent was anywhere
else.

34
00:04:38,527 --> 00:04:49,170
Hebron, where he would die and be buried with Abraham, is about a day's walk at one
o'clock. Jerusalem is about two days further on the same line.

35
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:59,497
And far beyond it, at the top of the map, is Haran, the city Rebekah came from,
about a month's walk for the son who is going to have to run there.

36
00:04:59,547 --> 00:05:06,900
Keep Beersheba and Haran in mind. The whole chapter happens in one tent, and it ends
with a road.

37
00:05:10,950 --> 00:05:14,260
Genesis chapter 27, verses 1 to 4.

38
00:05:14,810 --> 00:05:28,272
[Reader] When Isaac was old and his eyes were so weak that he could no longer see,
he called his older son Esau and said to him, “My son.” “Here I am,” Esau replied.

39
00:05:28,322 --> 00:05:41,709
[Reader] “Look,” said Isaac, “I am now old, and I do not know the day of my death.
Take your weapons—your quiver and bow—and go out into the field to hunt some game
for me.

40
00:05:41,759 --> 00:05:48,840
[Reader] Then prepare a tasty dish that I love and bring it to me to eat, so that I
may bless you before I die.”

41
00:05:49,890 --> 00:05:58,455
I do not know the day of my death. He had, as it turned out, about forty years left.

42
00:05:58,505 --> 00:06:11,480
But he could not see, and he thought the end was near, and he wanted to do the one
thing a father in that family did before he died: put the blessing on the firstborn.

43
00:06:11,530 --> 00:06:19,880
What he did not know was that someone else was in earshot. Verse 5 says Rebekah was
listening.

44
00:06:19,930 --> 00:06:31,680
The plan she made in the next few minutes is worth hearing in the text's own words,
because every piece of it is aimed at one of her husband's senses. Verses 5 to 17.

45
00:06:32,230 --> 00:06:37,086
[Reader] Now Rebekah was listening to what Isaac told his son Esau.

46
00:06:37,136 --> 00:06:48,092
[Reader] So when Esau went into the field to hunt game and bring it back, Rebekah
said to her son Jacob, “Behold, I overheard your father saying to your brother Esau,

47
00:06:48,142 --> 00:06:56,645
[Reader] ‘Bring me some game and prepare me a tasty dish to eat, so that I may bless
you in the presence of the LORD before I die.’

48
00:06:56,695 --> 00:07:01,820
[Reader] Now, my son, listen to my voice and do exactly as I tell you.

49
00:07:01,870 --> 00:07:11,096
[Reader] Go out to the flock and bring me two choice young goats, so that I can make
them into a tasty dish for your father—the kind he loves.

50
00:07:11,146 --> 00:07:16,662
[Reader] Then take it to your father to eat, so that he may bless you before he
dies.”

51
00:07:16,712 --> 00:07:25,058
[Reader] Jacob answered his mother Rebekah, “Look, my brother Esau is a hairy man,
but I am smooth-skinned. What if my father touches me?

52
00:07:25,108 --> 00:07:37,596
[Reader] Then I would be revealed to him as a deceiver, and I would bring upon
myself a curse rather than a blessing.” His mother replied, “Your curse be on me, my
son.

53
00:07:37,646 --> 00:07:48,017
[Reader] Just obey my voice and go get them for me.” So Jacob went and got two goats
and brought them to his mother, who made the tasty food his father loved.

54
00:07:48,067 --> 00:07:56,487
[Reader] And Rebekah took the finest clothes in the house that belonged to her older
son Esau, and she put them on her younger son Jacob.

55
00:07:56,537 --> 00:08:06,660
[Reader] She also put the skins of the young goats on his hands and on the smooth
part of his neck. Then she handed her son Jacob the tasty food and bread she had
made.

56
00:08:07,510 --> 00:08:16,697
Notice who raises the problem of touch. Not Rebekah. Jacob. What if my father
touches me?

57
00:08:16,747 --> 00:08:23,527
He knows his father is blind, and he knows what a blind man does instead of looking.

58
00:08:23,577 --> 00:08:33,877
So the goat kids are killed for two purposes: the meat for Isaac's tongue, the skins
for his hands. The clothes are for his nose.

59
00:08:33,927 --> 00:08:42,900
Only his ears are left to him, and the plan has nothing for those. Now the tent.
Verses 18 to 29.

60
00:08:43,150 --> 00:08:56,629
[Reader] So Jacob went to his father and said, “My father.” “Here I am!” he
answered. “Which one are you, my son?” Jacob said to his father, “I am Esau, your
firstborn.

61
00:08:56,679 --> 00:09:08,806
[Reader] I have done as you told me. Please sit up and eat some of my game, so that
you may bless me.” But Isaac asked his son, “How did you ever find it so quickly, my
son?”

62
00:09:08,856 --> 00:09:19,078
[Reader] “Because the LORD your God brought it to me,” he replied. Then Isaac said
to Jacob, “Please come closer so I can touch you, my son.

63
00:09:19,128 --> 00:09:23,006
[Reader] Are you really my son Esau, or not?”

64
00:09:23,056 --> 00:09:34,158
[Reader] So Jacob came close to his father Isaac, who touched him and said, “The
voice is the voice of Jacob, but the hands are the hands of Esau.”

65
00:09:34,208 --> 00:09:45,954
[Reader] Isaac did not recognize him, because his hands were hairy like those of his
brother Esau; so he blessed him. Again he asked, “Are you really my son Esau?”

66
00:09:46,004 --> 00:09:56,505
[Reader] And he replied, “I am.” “Serve me,” said Isaac, “and let me eat some of my
son’s game, so that I may bless you.”

67
00:09:56,555 --> 00:10:07,647
[Reader] Jacob brought it to him, and he ate; then he brought him wine, and he
drank. Then his father Isaac said to him, “Please come near and kiss me, my son.”

68
00:10:07,697 --> 00:10:10,045
[Reader] So he came near and kissed him.

69
00:10:10,095 --> 00:10:19,998
[Reader] When Isaac smelled his clothing, he blessed him and said: “Ah, the smell of
my son is like the smell of a field that the LORD has blessed.

70
00:10:20,048 --> 00:10:31,677
[Reader] May God give to you the dew of heaven and the richness of the earth—an
abundance of grain and new wine. May peoples serve you and nations bow down to you.

71
00:10:31,727 --> 00:10:42,380
[Reader] May you be the master of your brothers, and may the sons of your mother bow
down to you. May those who curse you be cursed, and those who bless you be blessed.”

72
00:10:43,430 --> 00:10:57,808
Count what he did. He asked which son it was. He asked how the hunt could be over so
soon. He asked to touch him. He said out loud that the voice was wrong.

73
00:10:57,858 --> 00:11:04,245
He asked a second time: are you really Esau? He ate.

74
00:11:04,295 --> 00:11:14,677
He called him close and kissed him, and only then, with the smell of Esau's clothes
in his face, did he give the blessing.

75
00:11:14,727 --> 00:11:24,898
Four senses out of five reported Esau, and three of them had been dressed for the
purpose. The one that had not been dressed was the one he overruled.

76
00:11:24,948 --> 00:11:32,905
And there was a witness he never called. The Lord had already said which of these
two boys the line would run through.

77
00:11:32,955 --> 00:11:38,740
Isaac checked everything he could reach with his body, and nothing he would have had
to take on faith.

78
00:11:40,250 --> 00:11:50,680
Now imagine the next few minutes. The text gives us the frame: Jacob had barely left
his father's presence when Esau came in.

79
00:11:51,520 --> 00:12:00,502
Isaac is alone, and he is content. He has eaten. He has done the thing he feared he
would die without doing.

80
00:12:00,552 --> 00:12:12,644
The blessing of Abraham has gone where it should go, to the firstborn, the son who
smells like the open country. He is tired in the good way.

81
00:12:12,694 --> 00:12:14,830
And then the matting at the door moves again.

82
00:12:18,130 --> 00:12:20,000
[Esau] My father.

83
00:12:19,677 --> 00:12:21,340
[Isaac] Who are you?

84
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:30,485
Who are you? The same two words he had just heard, in a different voice, and this
time he does not know it.

85
00:12:30,535 --> 00:12:37,781
A few minutes earlier his ears had picked Jacob out through a disguise. Nothing has
gone wrong with them since.

86
00:12:37,831 --> 00:12:47,483
What has changed is that he has just blessed Esau and sent him out, so whoever is at
the door now cannot be Esau.

87
00:12:47,533 --> 00:12:54,548
That is the sound of a man's expectation overruling his ears for the second time in
one hour,

88
00:12:54,598 --> 00:13:00,245
and it is the last thing he hears before the floor goes out from under him.

89
00:13:00,295 --> 00:13:01,830
Verses 30 to 38.

90
00:13:03,860 --> 00:13:12,807
[Reader] As soon as Isaac had finished blessing him and Jacob had left his father’s
presence, his brother Esau returned from the hunt.

91
00:13:12,857 --> 00:13:22,919
[Reader] He too made some tasty food, brought it to his father, and said to him, “My
father, sit up and eat of your son’s game, so that you may bless me.”

92
00:13:22,969 --> 00:13:31,594
[Reader] But his father Isaac replied, “Who are you?” “I am Esau, your firstborn
son,” he answered.

93
00:13:31,644 --> 00:13:38,277
[Reader] Isaac began to tremble violently and said, “Who was it, then, who hunted
the game and brought it to me?

94
00:13:38,327 --> 00:13:44,531
[Reader] Before you came in, I ate it all and blessed him—and indeed, he will be
blessed!”

95
00:13:44,581 --> 00:13:54,124
[Reader] When Esau heard his father’s words, he let out a loud and bitter cry and
said to his father, “Bless me too, O my father!”

96
00:13:54,174 --> 00:14:06,520
[Reader] But Isaac replied, “Your brother came deceitfully and took your blessing.”
So Esau declared, “Is he not rightly named Jacob? For he has cheated me twice.

97
00:14:06,570 --> 00:14:14,882
[Reader] He took my birthright, and now he has taken my blessing.” Then he asked,
“Haven’t you saved a blessing for me?”

98
00:14:14,932 --> 00:14:27,008
[Reader] But Isaac answered Esau: “Look, I have made him your master and given him
all his relatives as servants; I have sustained him with grain and new wine.

99
00:14:27,058 --> 00:14:41,090
[Reader] What is left that I can do for you, my son?” Esau said to his father, “Do
you have only one blessing, my father? Bless me too, O my father!” Then Esau wept
aloud.

100
00:14:42,140 --> 00:14:54,789
Tremble violently. The Hebrew says he trembled a great trembling, greatly, and the
translators have struggled with the pile of words ever since.

101
00:14:54,839 --> 00:15:01,198
It is the moment a blind man learns that the room was not what he was told it was.

102
00:15:01,248 --> 00:15:09,407
Everything he had checked, every question he had asked, had been answered by people
who knew exactly what he would check.

103
00:15:09,457 --> 00:15:22,090
And then the strangest sentence in the chapter: and indeed, he will be blessed. He
does not take it back. He could have. He says it stands.

104
00:15:23,640 --> 00:15:25,510
Brian, what do you hear in this one?

105
00:15:26,060 --> 00:15:35,669
[Brian] I hear trust, two kinds of it. The trust we place in the people around us,
which gets heavier as the senses go. Isaac did everything right.

106
00:15:35,719 --> 00:15:44,836
[Brian] He asked, he listened, he reached out, he smelled the clothes, and the
people he trusted had prepared for every one of those. And an older trust.

107
00:15:44,886 --> 00:15:54,272
[Brian] This is the man who had been the lamb on Moriah, who prayed twenty years for
his wife. He had been told his whole life to trust the Lord, and mostly he had.

108
00:15:54,322 --> 00:16:03,073
[Brian] When I first read this, my thought was that Isaac's sense of touch must have
failed along with his sight, because how else could goatskin pass for a man's hands?

109
00:16:03,123 --> 00:16:13,386
[Brian] I have changed my mind, and the reason is my own hands. Mine sit in
conditioned air all day on a keyboard. My wife Barb says they are soft as a baby's
bottom.

110
00:16:13,436 --> 00:16:21,666
[Brian] Isaac's were not. He had spent a hundred years outdoors among flocks and
tents and wells, doing hard work for meagre provision.

111
00:16:21,716 --> 00:16:28,482
[Brian] Hands like that do not feel fine detail the way mine do. Josephus says the
twins differed only in their hairiness.

112
00:16:28,532 --> 00:16:36,794
[Brian] Put a goatskin under a hand like Isaac's and it is not the stretch it
sounds. It helps to remember where he was. Beersheba was his place.

113
00:16:36,844 --> 00:16:42,260
[Brian] He had reopened his father's well there and named it, and he was still there
decades later when Jacob left.

114
00:16:42,310 --> 00:16:49,488
[Brian] A blind man in a camp he has known for half a lifetime is a different man
from one set down in a strange room.

115
00:16:49,538 --> 00:16:56,359
[Brian] He would have known his tent, his way to the door, who usually came and
went. That is part of why the deceit worked.

116
00:16:56,409 --> 00:17:02,710
[Brian] Nothing about the day was strange except the one thing that mattered. The
detail that stays with me is Esau's voice.

117
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:12,059
[Brian] A few minutes earlier Isaac had picked out Jacob's voice through a disguise,
so his hearing was fine. Yet when Esau walked in, Isaac asked, "Who are you?"

118
00:17:12,109 --> 00:17:21,734
[Brian] Nothing was wrong with his ears. What had changed was what he expected. He
had just blessed Esau and sent him out, so the man coming in could not be Esau.

119
00:17:21,784 --> 00:17:31,782
[Brian] I call this context. My ears tell me a great deal, but what I expect to hear
tells me almost as much, and when the two disagree, expectation often wins.

120
00:17:31,832 --> 00:17:34,010
[Brian] It won twice in one hour for Isaac.

121
00:17:37,289 --> 00:17:46,439
[Brian] People ask me how I can trust anyone, living like this. The honest answer is
that I have no choice, and neither did Isaac, and neither do you.

122
00:17:46,489 --> 00:17:50,442
[Brian] You just do not notice, because your eyes let you pretend you checked.

123
00:17:50,492 --> 00:17:57,986
[Brian] I check with everything I have, the way he did, and then I take the word of
the person in the room. Most days that is enough.

124
00:17:58,036 --> 00:18:03,962
[Brian] This chapter is the Bible's record of the day it was not, and the Bible does
not blame the blind man for it.

125
00:18:04,012 --> 00:18:10,447
[Brian] When the people he trusted failed him, Isaac trembled, and then he said, he
shall be blessed.

126
00:18:10,497 --> 00:18:19,573
[Brian] He had been trying to give the blessing to the son he favoured, against the
word spoken to Rebekah. When he understood, he took the Lord's choice over his own.

127
00:18:19,623 --> 00:18:28,470
[Brian] Sight is the sense that lets a person believe he walked by sight. Every
blind man knows he walks by something else. This chapter is about which something
else.

128
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:35,933
Now the old books, and this time one of them was written by a blind man.

129
00:18:35,983 --> 00:18:44,072
William Hanks Levy, who lost his sight as a young man and wrote a book on blindness
and the blind in London in 1872,

130
00:18:44,122 --> 00:18:51,790
put Isaac among his biographies of the blind and said his readers would be struck by
one thing in the chapter.

131
00:18:52,340 --> 00:18:56,026
[The old books] The intelligent use made by Isaac of his hearing, touch, and smell,

132
00:18:56,076 --> 00:19:03,330
[The old books] which is the more surprising as it might naturally be supposed that
by reason of age these senses would have become almost useless.

133
00:19:04,360 --> 00:19:15,392
And at the end of the passage he adds that the sagacity Isaac showed would have been
almost enough, under ordinary circumstances, to find the fraud out.

134
00:19:15,442 --> 00:19:20,956
A blind man read it and saw a careful blind man.

135
00:19:21,006 --> 00:19:33,675
Matthew Henry, a century and a half earlier, read it as a sighted man, and he went
sense by sense through what Rebekah did: his taste, with kid dressed as venison;

136
00:19:33,725 --> 00:19:38,044
his touch and his smell, with the clothes and the skins.

137
00:19:38,094 --> 00:19:42,630
The one she could not touch was his hearing, and Henry stopped there.

138
00:19:43,180 --> 00:19:52,310
[Matthew Henry] Isaac was at first dissatisfied, and would have discovered the fraud
if he could have trusted his own ears; for the voice was Jacob's voice.

139
00:19:52,360 --> 00:19:59,974
[Matthew Henry] Providence has ordered a strange variety of voices as well as faces,
which is also of use to prevent our being imposed upon;

140
00:20:00,024 --> 00:20:03,930
[Matthew Henry] and the voice is a thing not easily disguised nor counterfeited.

141
00:20:05,480 --> 00:20:12,500
If he could have trusted his own ears. He could have. He chose the hands.

142
00:20:13,288 --> 00:20:28,150
Now the word. Each of these six stories has its own word for eyes that fail, and
they make a chain. Last time the word was "ah-VAR," to blind, a thing done to a man.

143
00:20:28,200 --> 00:20:43,053
This time it is a thing that happens to him: "kah-HAH." To grow dim, to grow dull,
to fade. Genesis 27 opens with it: his eyes were dim from seeing.

144
00:20:43,103 --> 00:20:52,944
It is the ordinary word for old eyes going, and that is what makes it worth
following, because the Bible uses it twice more of old men,

145
00:20:52,994 --> 00:20:55,435
and once to say it did not happen.

146
00:20:55,485 --> 00:21:06,306
The second old man is Eli, at Shiloh, two episodes from now: his eyes had begun to
grow dim, the same word, so that he could not see.

147
00:21:06,356 --> 00:21:18,678
The third is in the book of Job, where Job says his eye has grown dim from grief,
the same word used of sorrow instead of years. And the one denial is Moses.

148
00:21:18,728 --> 00:21:30,000
Deuteronomy says that at a hundred and twenty his eye was not dim, this exact word
with a not in front of it, and his strength had not left him.

149
00:21:30,050 --> 00:21:39,967
The Bible knows the difference between an old man whose eyes went and an old man
whose eyes did not, and it uses one word for both.

150
00:21:40,017 --> 00:21:47,118
Isaac is the first in the chain who simply grew old. Zedekiah's blindness was done
to him.

151
00:21:47,168 --> 00:21:56,760
Isaac's came the way it comes to most people, and the chapter never treats it as a
judgment. It is just the fact the whole family built their plan around.

152
00:21:59,810 --> 00:22:07,031
What came after. Esau swore to kill his brother once the mourning for their father
was over.

153
00:22:07,081 --> 00:22:13,952
Rebekah heard of it and sent Jacob to her brother in Haran, telling Isaac it was to
find a wife.

154
00:22:14,002 --> 00:22:26,408
And then Isaac did something the chapter we read does not prepare you for. He called
Jacob in and blessed him again, this time knowing exactly who he was.

155
00:22:26,458 --> 00:22:35,579
The New Testament remembers that second blessing, in the chapter that lists the
people who lived by faith. The chapter opens with a definition.

156
00:22:35,629 --> 00:22:42,800
Hebrews chapter 11, verse 1, and then, skipping to the one verse it gives to Isaac,
verse 20.

157
00:22:43,370 --> 00:22:54,920
[Reader] Now faith is the assurance of what we hope for and the certainty of what we
do not see. By faith Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau concerning the future.

158
00:22:56,720 --> 00:23:10,795
The certainty of what we do not see, and then a blind man blessing. That verse is
the only thing from Isaac's hundred and eighty years that Hebrews names. Not Moriah.

159
00:23:10,845 --> 00:23:25,070
Not the wells. Not the twenty years of prayer. This. By faith, not by the hands this
time, and not by the voice. Genesis chapter 28, verses 3 and 4.

160
00:23:26,220 --> 00:23:35,185
[Reader] May God Almighty bless you and make you fruitful and multiply you, so that
you may become a company of peoples.

161
00:23:35,235 --> 00:23:46,090
[Reader] And may He give the blessing of Abraham to you and your descendants, so
that you may possess the land where you dwell as a foreigner, the land God gave to
Abraham.”

162
00:23:47,240 --> 00:23:59,973
The blessing of Abraham, given on purpose this time, to the son who had taken it by
trick. Isaac could not see Jacob's face then either. He did not need to.

163
00:24:00,023 --> 00:24:04,967
He knew which one he was. Jacob was gone twenty years.

164
00:24:05,017 --> 00:24:13,430
When he came home he found his father still alive, at Hebron, and Genesis chapter 35
ends the story in two verses.

165
00:24:13,930 --> 00:24:27,800
[Reader] And Isaac lived 180 years. Then he breathed his last and died and was
gathered to his people, old and full of years. And his sons Esau and Jacob buried
him.

166
00:24:28,600 --> 00:24:34,543
His sons Esau and Jacob. Both of them, together, at one grave.

167
00:24:34,593 --> 00:24:41,481
The man who did not know the day of his death lived another forty years without his
sight,

168
00:24:41,531 --> 00:24:48,883
long enough for the brothers who had torn his house in half to stand side by side at
his burial.

169
00:24:48,933 --> 00:24:54,230
Nothing in Genesis says his eyes came back. Nothing says he needed them to.

170
00:24:55,530 --> 00:24:57,560
Brian, with the last word.

171
00:24:58,110 --> 00:25:05,363
[Brian] The first time, Isaac checked everything he could. Hands, smell, taste, the
food, the voice.

172
00:25:05,413 --> 00:25:15,111
[Brian] He did all of that and still got it wrong, because the people he was
checking had planned for every check. The second time he did not check anything. He
could not.

173
00:25:15,161 --> 00:25:23,544
[Brian] He knew who was standing there, and he knew what the Lord had said, and he
blessed him on that. That is what trust looks like when sight is gone.

174
00:25:23,594 --> 00:25:29,787
[Brian] You check what you can, and then you take what you are given, from the
people in the room and from the Lord.

175
00:25:29,837 --> 00:25:38,700
[Brian] Isaac found out that it is the best way to live, and the Bible calls that
faith. And I think that applies to all of us. Regardless of what we see.

176
00:25:42,450 --> 00:25:53,317
Next time, the son. Jacob, who stood in his father's tent in a goatskin, grows old
in Egypt, and his own eyes go.

177
00:25:53,367 --> 00:26:05,845
Joseph brings two boys to him for a blessing, and the old man who once fooled a
blind father reaches out, and crosses his hands. Hebrews gives him the very next
verse.

178
00:26:05,895 --> 00:26:08,960
Jacob, and the blessing of Joseph's sons.

179
00:26:12,209 --> 00:26:20,172
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

180
00:26:20,222 --> 00:26:30,956
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

181
00:26:31,006 --> 00:26:38,200
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

